h1 Practices
card
  image https://www.w3.org/WAI/content-images/wai-aria-practices/images/read-this-first.svg "Illustration of a brown-skinned woman with a slight smile gesturing towards the right with her hand"
  h2 Read This First
  text No ARIA is better than Bad ARIA. Before using any ARIA, .
  link "Read this to understand why no ARIA is better than bad ARIA" /WAI/content-assets/wai-aria-practices/../../ARIA/apg/practices/read-me-first/
row
  col
    link "Landmark Regions" landmark-regions/
    text ARIA landmark roles provide a powerful way to identify the organization and structure of a web page.
  col
    link "Providing Accessible Names and Descriptions" names-and-descriptions/
    text Providing elements with accessible names, and where appropriate, accessible descriptions, is one of the most important responsibilities authors have when developing accessible web experiences.
  col
    link "Developing a Keyboard Interface" keyboard-interface/
    text Unlike native HTML form elements, browsers do not provide keyboard support for graphical user interface (GUI) components that are made accessible with ARIA; authors have to provide the keyboard support in their code.
  col
    link "Grid and Table Properties" grid-and-table-properties/
    text To fully present and describe a grid or table, in addition to parsing the headers, rows, and cells using the roles described in the grid pattern or table pattern, assistive technologies need to be able to determine other structural and presentation characteristics, such as the number and visibility of rows and columns.
  col
    link "Communicating Value and Limits for Range Widgets" range-related-properties/
    text ARIA defines the following roles as range widgets, which means they communicate a value that is typically numeric and constrained to defined limits.
  col
    link "Structural Roles" structural-roles/
    text ARIA provides a set of roles that convey the accessibility semantics of structures on a page.
  col
    link "Hiding Semantics with the presentation Role" hiding-semantics/
    text While ARIA is primarily used to express semantics, there are some situations where hiding an element’s semantics from assistive technologies is helpful.
